.TH SET_INTERSECTION 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
set_intersection \- Compute the intersection of two sets

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/set.h>

.IB int
.IB set_intersection(set_t* out, const set_t* a, const set_t* b);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBset_intersection\fR function computes the intersection of two sets, storing the result in the third set. It takes three arguments: the set to store the result, and the two sets to compute the intersection of. The function returns the number of elements in the intersection.

.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IB out
The set to store the intersection of the two sets.
.TP
.IB a
The first set to compute the intersection of.
.TP
.IB b
The second set to compute the intersection of.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The function returns the number of elements in the intersection of the two sets.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBset_intersection\fR function:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/set.h>

int main() {
    set_t set1, set2, result;
    set_init(&set1);
    set_init(&set2);
    set_init(&result);

    set_inserts(&set1, "apple");
    set_inserts(&set1, "banana");
    set_inserts(&set2, "banana");
    set_inserts(&set2, "cherry");

    int count = set_intersection(&result, &set1, &set2);

    printf("Intersection contains %d elements:\n", count);
    set_iterator_t it;
    const char* x;
    size_t n;
    set_foreach(&result, it, x, n) {
        printf("%.*s\n", (int)n, x);
    }

    set_free(&set1);
    set_free(&set2);
    set_free(&result);
    return 0;
}
.nef